</td></tr></table></td></tr></table>

<table border="0" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" width="100%"><tr><td>
<a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=23"><img src="images/topics/look.gif" border="0" Alt="Look" align="right" hspace="10" vspace="10"></a>
<div align=justify><font class="posted" color="#000000"><P><SPAN id=a10bl><I>November 26, 2003</I> -- <!--start bodytext--><B>SEYMOUR</B> <B>Stein</B>, art collector? It's true. While Stein is best known as the Brooklynite who discovered the Ramones, Talking Heads, <B>Madonna</B>, etc., and signed them to his Sire Records, he has also been quietly collecting art for 35 years. On Dec. 11, he's selling much of his collection at Sotheby's, from Ch'ing Dynasty porcelain and Art Deco furniture to Pre-Raphaelite paintings. Stein, whose ex-wife <B>Linda</B> is a top<B></B> real estate broker, explained in the catalog: "Over the years, most of the wonderful things I bought lay in storage in Paris, London and New York - many still in their original shipping crates. This is never what I intended to happen." The prize of the collection is a chromium-plated aluminum bed made in the early 1930s by Louis Sognot and Charlotte Alix for the Maharaja of Indore for his Manik Bagh Palace. It's estimated to go for $180,000-$240,000. The whole collection is valued at $5 million. <STRONG>Madonna</STRONG>, in a blurb in the catalog, said Stein's apartments in New York and London "were more like warehouses stacked with paintings and porcelain like something out of 'Citizen Kane.' " Said one rock writer: "His eyes are as good as his ears." </SPAN></P>
<P><SPAN>I wonder if Madonna will buy some of his art. I know she really loves Pre-Raphaelite art.</SPAN></P>
<P><SPAN><A href="http://nypost.com">http://nypost.com</A></P></SPAN>
</font></div>
</td></tr></table>


</td></tr></table>


